well another day and another major  legislative accomplishment for joe biden  whose presidency in the last two months  as i told you about yesterday and the  day before  has really turned itself around the  inflation reduction act has now been  signed into law by president joe biden  this bill you might recall  barely passed the u.s senate 5150 but  sir how could it add up to 101 votes  well it was 50 50 with kamala harris as  vice president is the president of the  senate casting the tie-breaking vote 51  that was on august 7th there were no  republican votes we also saw the bill on  friday past the u.s house not  particularly widely by a 220 to 207  margin and joe biden has now signed the  bill into law and it is indeed major  major legislation let's take a look at  what this bill does it is a more than  430 billion dollar package expected to  reduce the deficit by over 300 billion  dollars over 10 years  it's expected to raise  737 billion dollars in revenue over 10  years and it includes 369 billion in  investment money for climate and energy  policy  64 billion dollars to extend the  affordable care act policy that reduces  health insurance costs so to be more  honest sometimes what it what we can  maybe more accurately say is it slows  down the rate of increase for health  insurance costs it includes importantly  notably a 15 percent corporate minimum  tax for companies earning over a billion  dollars a year this is a subject of much  discussion  which is  for individuals there has been an  alternative minimum tax for a long time  and the concept of an alternative  minimum tax is you make a million  dollars personally  you can use deductions and losses and  all these different things  to offset the full million in taxes and  you end up owing zero in order to  prevent that something called the  alternative minimum tax was established  which says that for a certain level of  income no matter how many deductions and  losses you have to offset taxable income  you have to pay some minimum tax we  never had that for corporations this  bill puts that in place at a 15 percent  rate it also puts in place a one percent  tax on stock buybacks done by large  corporations this was something that was  a big deal during the pandemic you may  recall  uh when the donald trump uh coronavirus  stimuli packages were  enacted  many corporations from the um corporate  side of it  used the money they got  to just buy back their own stock raising  its price so this bill puts in place a  one percent tax when you do stock  buybacks it will reduce drug prices for  medicare recipients uh it will it  expects to collect an additional 124  billion dollars from increased irs  enforcement so there's money in the bill  controversially so for many to hire more  irs agents the belief is that those  additional agents are going to find tax  cheats and recover 124 billion  additional dollars it includes tax  credits to incentivize electric cars and  electric hvac systems as well as other  forms of cleaner technology that's great  and it is expected to cut pollution by  around 40 percent below the 2005 level  by 2030  and it also puts in place a 2 000 a year  out-of-pocket cap  for medicare recipients now a few  thoughts on this number one  is this a major legislative  accomplishment for joe biden yes it is  will people vote based on this in  november it's not totally clear  um  we'll see what polling suggests over the  next probably two to three weeks  regardless of your political persuasion  there are things in this that are  beneficial to most of the americans uh  in the country  for example the two thousand dollar  out-of-pocket cap for medicare  recipients medicare recipients are both  democrats and republicans in fact  we it's a more conservative group we  know that every older cohort is slightly  more conservative in the united states  right now  less pollution is everybody benefits  from less pollution even if you're  against  green energy or whatever 